   <dc:title>
    <rdf:Alt>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">In Sync: Memory Similarity and Recall Order for the 2017 Eurovision Song Contest</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">In Sync: Memory Similarity and Recall Order for the 2017 Eurovision Song Contest</rdf:li>
    </rdf:Alt>
   </dc:title>
   <dc:subject>
    <rdf:Bag>
     <rdf:li>memory similarity; shared memory; event memory; public event; recall order</rdf:li>
    </rdf:Bag>
   </dc:subject>
   <dc:type>
    <rdf:Bag>
     <rdf:li>Text</rdf:li>
    </rdf:Bag>
   </dc:type>
   <dc:description>
    <rdf:Alt>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">When recalling a widely shared public event</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">When recalling a widely shared public event</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">individuals tend to retrieve similar details in their narratives. However</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">individuals tend to retrieve similar details in their narratives. However</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">the extent to which memory representations converge across individuals and whether recollections follow a systematic order remain unclear. In this study</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">the extent to which memory representations converge across individuals and whether recollections follow a systematic order remain unclear. In this study</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">we examined the extent to which memory representations of Portugalâ€™s victory in the 2017 Eurovision Song Contest are shared across individuals</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">we examined the extent to which memory representations of Portugalâ€™s victory in the 2017 Eurovision Song Contest are shared across individuals</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">whether recollections follow systematic order patterns</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">whether recollections follow systematic order patterns</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">and which determinants are associated with event memory accuracy and inter-subject memory similarity. Participants (N = 58) reported their memories of the event approximately 6.5 years after it occurred. Representational similarity analyses showed substantial overlap in recalled details across participants</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">and which determinants are associated with event memory accuracy and inter-subject memory similarity. Participants (N = 58) reported their memories of the event approximately 6.5 years after it occurred. Representational similarity analyses showed substantial overlap in recalled details across participants</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">indicating convergence in collective memory. Regression analyses showed that greater media exposure was associated with higher event memory accuracy</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">indicating convergence in collective memory. Regression analyses showed that greater media exposure was associated with higher event memory accuracy</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">but we found no clear evidence that the reported memory determinants were associated with similarity across participantsâ€™ narratives. Analyses of recall order revealed a consistent sequential pattern</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">but we found no clear evidence that the reported memory determinants were associated with similarity across participantsâ€™ narratives. Analyses of recall order revealed a consistent sequential pattern</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">with the song title immediately following the singerâ€™s name. The observed order may reflect the frequent co-presentation of these details in media coverage. Together</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">with the song title immediately following the singerâ€™s name. The observed order may reflect the frequent co-presentation of these details in media coverage. Together</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">these findings suggest that collective memories for public events are characterised by some shared content</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">these findings suggest that collective memories for public events are characterised by some shared content</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">but also by an organised recall pattern that reflects shared cultural knowledge and associative memory processes.</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">but also by an organised recall pattern that reflects shared cultural knowledge and associative memory processes.</rdf:li>
    </rdf:Alt>
   </dc:description>
